            "id": "S10_G",
            "passage": "Every Chinese New Year, Great-Aunt Lian made her famous pineapple tarts. People said they were the best in the whole neighbourhood. Families would come from nearby streets just to get a small tin of them.\n\nThis year, Great-Aunt Lian was eighty years old. Her hands shook a little as she rolled the pastry, but her eyes were sharp and her instructions were clear. Twelve-year-old Nadia sat beside her, watching every movement carefully.\n\n'The secret,' said Great-Aunt Lian, 'is not the recipe. It is the patience.' She showed Nadia how to press each small circle of pastry gently, how to place just the right amount of filling in the centre, and how to seal the edges without cracking them.\n\nBy the afternoon, they made three hundred tarts. Nadia's back ached and her fingers were sticky with sugar, but she did not complain. She was too busy learning.\n\n'You have good hands,' said Great-Aunt Lian at the end of the day. 'Next year, you can make them yourself.'\n\nNadia felt a warmth spread through her chest. She realised that Great-Aunt Lian was not just teaching her to bake. She was passing down something that words alone could not explain.",
