            "passage": "Eleven-year-old Priya wanted to be an astronaut from the age of four. Her bedroom ceiling was covered with glow-in-the-dark stars, and her bookshelf held more books about space than about anything else.\n\nOne evening, her school organised a visit to the Science Centre for a special planetarium show. Priya arrived thirty minutes early, claiming a seat in the very front row. While the other students chatted and played on their phones, Priya sat perfectly still, staring up at the dark dome above her.\n\nWhen the show began, the dome lit up with thousands of simulated stars. The narrator explained how stars are born inside giant clouds of gas and dust called nebulae. Priya leaned forward in her seat, her notebook open on her lap. She wrote down every fact, every name and every number.\n\nAfterward, a scientist named Dr Ariff stayed to answer questions. One by one, the other students gave short answers and walked away. But Priya stayed. She asked about the temperature at the core of a star, how long it takes light to travel from the nearest star to Earth and whether any current missions were searching for planets like ours.\n\nDr Ariff looked at her with a wide smile. 'You know,' he said, 'I have been visiting schools for fifteen years. I have never met a student quite like you.'\n\nPriya did not know what to say. She just looked back up at the dome, which was now dark and still. She smiled quietly to herself.",
