            "id": "S24_G",
            "passage": "Of all the creatures in the ocean, the seahorse is one of the most unusual. It does not look like a typical fish. It has a horse-like head, a long curved neck and a body covered in bony rings instead of scales. Its eyes move independently of each other, which means it can look in two different directions at the same time.\n\nSeahorses are very slow swimmers. They move upright through the water by fluttering their tiny dorsal fin very fast — up to fifty times per second. They use their curling tails to grip onto seagrass and coral so that strong currents do not sweep them away.\n\nOne of the most surprising facts about seahorses is that it is the male — not the female — that carries and gives birth to the young. The female deposits her eggs into a special pouch on the male's body. The male carries the eggs for several weeks until the baby seahorses are ready to swim out on their own.\n\nSeahorses are also skilled hunters. They creep up slowly behind tiny shrimps and then snap them up with their long tube-like mouths so fast that the shrimp barely has time to react.\n\nSadly, seahorses are under threat. They are caught in fishing nets, collected for use in traditional medicine and lose their homes when seagrass meadows and coral reefs are destroyed. Conservation groups are working hard to protect them and restore the habitats they depend on.",
