            "id": "S42_G",
            "passage": "Learning a new skill is one of the most rewarding experiences a person can have, but it rarely follows a straight line. Research shows that skill development typically happens in three stages.\n\nIn the first stage, everything feels clumsy and slow. A beginner has to think about every step — where to place their fingers, how to hold a tool, when to apply pressure. This conscious effort is tiring and mistakes are frequent.\n\nIn the second stage, some actions start to feel more natural. The learner no longer has to think about every small movement. This is when practice becomes most valuable, because the brain is beginning to form reliable pathways for the new skill.\n\nIn the third stage, the skill feels automatic. An expert pianist does not think about individual notes — they feel the music. A skilled chef does not measure every ingredient — they sense when something is right. At this stage, the learner has moved from thinking to feeling.\n\nWhat makes the difference between those who reach stage three and those who stop at stage one? Research points to one factor above all others: the willingness to tolerate the discomfort of being a beginner.",
