            "passage": "Thirteen-year-old Dev watched his grandmother bake bread for as long as he could remember. Her hands moved without measuring — a pinch of this, a handful of that. Dev had always wondered if he could do the same.\n\nOne Saturday morning, he decided to try. He found the recipe card in the back of the recipe book — her handwriting, faded but legible. He followed every step. He measured. He kneaded for eight minutes by the clock. He let the dough rise for an hour.\n\nThe result was dense and flat. Nothing like his grandmother's bread.\n\nHe said nothing at dinner. But his grandmother noticed. After the dishes were cleared, she came and sat across from him.\n\n'You tried the bread,' she said. It was not a question.\n\nDev nodded. 'It was wrong,' he said. 'The recipe is the same but mine didn't come out like yours.'\n\nHis grandmother smiled. 'The recipe is the same. But you cannot bake it the way I do yet. I've made that bread four hundred times. Every time teaches you something the recipe doesn't say.'\n\nDev looked at his hands. 'So I just have to keep making it?' he asked.\n\n'Yes,' she said. 'And one day your bread will taste different from mine. Not worse. Just yours.'",
