            "id": "S48_G",
            "passage": "Singapore experiences thunderstorms more than 170 days a year, making it one of the world's most storm-prone countries. Understanding what happens during a thunderstorm and how to stay safe can make a real difference.\n\nA thunderstorm forms when warm, moist air rises rapidly and meets cooler air high in the atmosphere. This creates large storm clouds that produce lightning and thunder. Lightning is a powerful electrical discharge, and the thunder we hear is the sound it makes as it heats the air around it. Because light travels faster than sound, we always see lightning before we hear thunder. Counting the seconds between the flash and the bang tells you how far away the storm is — roughly one kilometre for every three seconds.\n\nDuring a thunderstorm, there are several safety rules to follow. You should stay indoors and away from windows. Avoid using electrical appliances and unplug devices where possible. If you are outdoors, do not shelter under a tree, as trees attract lightning. Move to a low-lying area instead and crouch down with your feet together and your hands over your ears.\n\nThunderstorms in Singapore usually pass within thirty to sixty minutes. After the rain stops, it is important to check for flooded paths and fallen branches before going outside.",
