            "passage": "Eight-year-old Zara never flew on a plane before this trip. She expected it to be loud, and it was. But it was also — unexpectedly — beautiful.\n\nFrom her window seat, she watched the ground disappear. Singapore shrank below her: highways became threads, buildings became squares, and the sea turned into a flat silver mirror. Then the clouds rose up around the plane, and she could no longer see the ground at all.\n\nShe pressed her nose against the cold glass and stared.\n\nHer father, who was sitting in the middle seat reading a magazine, glanced up. 'You all right?'\n\n'Dad,' said Zara quietly, 'is this what the world looks like from up here all the time?'\n\nHer father smiled. 'Every time,' he said.\n\nZara turned back to the window. Below her, the clouds formed a white landscape. Hills of cloud. Plains of cloud. A whole other world above the one she knew from the ground.\n\nShe did not touch her in-flight meal. She did not use the entertainment screen. She sat with her face turned to the window for the entire four-hour flight, watching the light change across the clouds as the sun moved slowly west.",
