            "id": "S58_G",
            "passage": "Every Saturday, nine-year-old Wei woke early and followed his grandfather to the wet market at Tekka. It was their ritual — just the two of them, before the rest of the family stirred.\n\nHis grandfather moved through the stalls like water, pausing here, nodding there, tasting a sliver of something before deciding. Wei knew enough to be patient. The market was not a place to rush.\n\nAt the fish stall, Uncle Raju always saved the best threadfin for their family. 'For you, Mr Lim — still swimming when it arrived this morning,' he would say, and grandfather would inspect it closely, turn it once, then nod.\n\nWei did not eat fish. But he liked the ceremony of it.\n\nAt the tofu stall, the auntie sliced quickly and silently — cubes of white, firm and soft, stacked in a green basin. She never needed to be told how much. She knew.\n\nOn the way home, grandfather carried the bags. Wei always asked to carry one, and grandfather always let him — the lightest one. Neither mentioned this.\n\nAt home, grandmother would begin to cook while grandfather washed the vegetables over the sink. Wei sat at the kitchen table, and the smell of garlic and old wood filled the flat.",
